bcc09 0.14.0
===========

bcc09 is a C compiler for the Motorola MC6809, with the preprocessor,
peephole optimizer, assembler, linker, librarian, binary utilities and
C library that go with it.  It targets the Tandy Color Computer and the
Dragon (Color BASIC, Disk BASIC and ROM cartridges), OS-9 and NitrOS-9,
FLEX, and the Vectrex, or any 6809 machine through its own start-up
code.  It is
derived from the 6809 configuration of Bruce Evans' C compiler, as the
dev86 project has it.

	https://github.com/retrotinker/bcc09

The tools
---------

	bcc09		the compiler driver: runs the passes below, and links
	as09		the assembler
	ld09		the linker, with linker scripts (as GNU ld's, smaller)
	ar09		the librarian
	objdump09	dumps object files and executables (also installed
			as nm09 and size09)
	objcopy09	makes an executable into what a machine loads: raw
			binary, S-records, Intel hex, a Disk BASIC (LOADM)
			file, a DragonDOS binary, a cassette image (.cas or
			.wav), an OS-9 module, or a FLEX command (.CMD)

bcc09's passes are cpp (the preprocessor), unproto (which makes ANSI C
prototypes into the K&R C the compiler reads, keeping their types),
cc1 (the compiler) and copt (the peephole optimizer, with -O).

The C it compiles is C89 with prototypes, less const and volatile (which
the headers define away), plus _Bool, and __direct for variables in the
6809's direct page (a byte smaller and a cycle faster to reach); char is
unsigned, int is 16 bits, long 32, float and double IEEE single and
double precision.  The C
library has string.h, ctype.h, stdlib.h (with malloc), stdarg.h, stdio.h
(printf and scanf as glibc's, floating point formats included), math.h
(Sun's fdlibm), stdint.h, stdbool.h, and the system functions each
target supports: files on Disk BASIC, OS-9 and FLEX, and directories on
OS-9.  Floating point is linked only into programs that use it.

Building and installing
-----------------------

bcc09 builds on any Unix-like system with a C compiler and make:

	make
	make check
	make install

"make check" runs the regression tests: the programs they build run on
sim6809, a 6809 simulator (https://github.com/retrotinker/sim6809, 1.2.0
or later; Fedora RPMs are attached to its releases), which must be on
PATH (or SIM6809=/path/to/sim6809).  The
optional "make check-xroar", "make check-nitros9" and "make check-flex"
run test programs on real ROMs and systems in the XRoar emulator, and
"make check-vectrex" on the Vectrex's BIOS in MAME (see the manual for
what each needs).

"make install" puts the programs in PREFIX/bin, the passes in
PREFIX/libexec/bcc09, the C library and its headers in
PREFIX/share/bcc09, and the man pages in PREFIX/share/man.  PREFIX is
/usr/local unless both make and make install are given another (bcc09
looks for its files where it was built to be installed): "make
PREFIX=/opt/bcc09" and "make PREFIX=/opt/bcc09 install", say.

On Fedora, "make rpm" builds the RPM packages (bcc09.spec, installed in
/usr): bcc09, and bcc09-doc with the PDF manuals, in rpmbuild/; "make
srpm" builds the source package.

Getting started
---------------

A Color BASIC program, on a cassette image for CLOADM:EXEC:

	bcc09 -Me -O -o hello -X-T0x3000 hello.c
	objcopy09 -O cas -n HELLO hello hello.cas

(or "objcopy09 -O decb hello HELLO.BIN" for LOADM from a disk).  A ROM
cartridge for the CoCo or the Dragon, its image ready for an EPROM (or
XRoar's -cart-rom):

	bcc09 -Mr -O -o hello.rom hello.c

A Vectrex cartridge, drawing through the BIOS's routines (vectrex.h):

	bcc09 -Mv -O -o hello.bin hello.c

An OS-9
program module, and a FLEX command:

	bcc09 -Mo -O -o hello.out hello.c
	objcopy09 -O os9 -n hello hello.out hello

	bcc09 -Mf -O -o hello -X-T0x1000 hello.c
	objcopy09 -O flex hello HELLO.CMD

For a monitor or a machine of your own (the default target has no I/O:
main's result is returned to whatever called the program):

	bcc09 -O -o prog -X-T0x4000 prog.c
	objcopy09 -O srec prog prog.s19

Color BASIC, OS-9 and FLEX end lines with a carriage return, and the C
library passes characters to them as they are: print "\r" (FLEX's
terminal wants "\r\n").

Documentation
-------------

doc/manual.pdf is the user's manual: building and installing, the C
bcc09 compiles, each target, the C library, the calling conventions and
how to write assembly for them, the tools and the tests, answers to
common questions (chapter 10, also on its own in FAQ.md), and the man
pages as an appendix ("make docs" makes it again from doc/manual.md).
doc/basic.pdf, "bcc09 for BASIC Programmers", teaches C to those who
know Color BASIC (from doc/basic.md), with doc/worm.c, an example game,
and doc/vectrex.pdf, "bcc09 for Vectrex Programmers", shows those who
know the Vectrex's BIOS and 6809 assembly how to write a cartridge in C
(from doc/vectrex.md), with doc/rocks.c, an example game.  The man pages are in doc/man: bcc09(1),
as09(1), ld09(1), ar09(1), objdump09(1), objcopy09(1), and the passes'
bcc09-cpp(1), bcc09-unproto(1), bcc09-cc1(1) and bcc09-copt(1) ("man -l
doc/man/bcc09.1" reads one in place).  libc/README describes the C
library and its changes from dev86's, BUGS the known bugs, and TODO
what is still to do.

Object formats
--------------

Syntax for the assembler seems to be inspired by (and may be compatible
with) that described in section C.5 of "INTROL-C COMPILER REFERENCE
MANUAL", available here:

	http://www.swtpc.com/mholley/Introl/REF.pdf

Many of the notes in section C.C seem relevant to BCC's 6809 code
generator as well, particular those on "REGISTER USAGE" and "PARAMETER
PASSING CONVENTIONS".  YMMV, of course...

The object file format produced by the assembler and consumed by the
linker is the "INTROL LINKABLE BINARY FILE FORMAT", documented in
"INTROL LINKER AND LOADER REFERENCE MANUAL", available here:

	http://www.swtpc.com/mholley/Introl/LOAD.pdf

So theoretically, object modules should be compatible with objects
generated by the Introl-C/6809 toolchain -- YMMV!  The calling
conventions would still need to match, of course.

License
-------

The compiler and tools are under the GNU General Public License (see
COPYING).  The C library, which is linked into the programs bcc09
builds, is under the GNU Library General Public License, version 2 (see
libc/COPYING.LIB), so that those programs can be under any license; its
math functions are Sun's fdlibm, under Sun's notice.

Please feel free to try things out and to let me know about your
successes or failures.  If you find something that does not compile or
which compiles incorrectly, I would welcome you to send me a minimal
source file that exhibits the problem along with a description of
what you are attempting to achieve.

